From b8d9e568b5151e7c3ec024822153716c6e4751f5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kel Modderman <kelvmod@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 18:15:37 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Don't let a script that holds the GIL keep Kodi from
 exiting

A script stuck in a C call that holds the GIL, such as a function called
through ctypes.PyDLL or a C extension that never releases it, keeps Kodi
from exiting. When Kodi stops, CPythonInvoker::stop() takes the GIL to
tell each script to abort, including scripts that have nothing to do
with the stuck one, and XBPython::Uninitialize() takes it to look for
what is left. Python has no way to wait for the GIL with a time limit.

Once Kodi is stopping, both now wait for the GIL on a helper thread for
up to 5 seconds, under a thread state of the main interpreter, which no
script can free. If the helper doesn't get it in time, Kodi gives up on
Python: the script being stopped is marked failed, as a script that
won't stop already is, later waits give up at once, stop() no longer
waits 5 seconds for scripts that can't run, and Python isn't finalized.
A helper that gets the GIL after it was given up on keeps it, so the
script that held it can't run on while Kodi shuts down. Before Kodi
stops nothing changes, except that stop() takes the GIL for the abort
notification under a thread state of the main interpreter instead of
the script's.

The abort notification runs no Python, but taking the GIL there finds a
stuck script before stop() waits 5 seconds for it. Using the main
interpreter for it also means stop() no longer leaves a thread state in
the script's interpreter while it waits, which ending that interpreter
would free on Python 3.14.

Raising SystemExit in a script still waits for the GIL as long as it
takes. It needs a thread state of the script's interpreter, which can't
wait on a helper, as ending the interpreter frees it, and can't be
swapped to after the wait, as PyThreadState_Swap() releases the GIL. So
a script that only starts holding the GIL after the abort notification
of a stop(), its own or another's, can still block there, as before.

XBPython::Uninitialize() no longer keeps the GIL on the main thread
itself. When scripts or interpreters are left, the helper keeps it
instead, and from then on the main thread must not take it. The only
place that would is ~XBPython(), which skips Py_Finalize() then. The
second Uninitialize() call now returns at once in both cases, as nothing
can change the outcome after the first one.

